EDIT_DESCRIPTION = (
    "Edit existing physical IFC occurrences atomically from one complete Parquet Package payload. "
    "mode=apply takes package_alias, payload_name and optional base_revision_ref; prefer the Package's unique "
    "system IFC source, otherwise copy source_revision.revision_ref from an actual current model/product read. "
    "Both sources must agree; a stale base conflicts rather than silently selecting new CURRENT. "
    "Each row identifies target_global_id and one action: replace with an exact published Family and complete inputs, "
    "placement to an absolute project-world rigid pose in metres, or delete its actual owned subtree. "
    "Read cad2bim://contracts/ifc_products_edit/input before constructing the rows. "
    "Targets are IfcElement occurrences including assemblies or internal components; do not target spatial nodes, "
    "Type definitions, ports or features independently. Duplicate/ancestor/overlapping targets reject the whole batch. "
    "No property/material/system allocation, nonuniform scale, automatic Family propagation or network reconnection is offered. "
    "Success returns the original report, whole-batch summary and committed revision; confirmed failure may have no report. "
    "Prepared/indeterminate has no terminal summary. A disconnect, missing response or retryable=false proves no failure or zero commit. "
    "Read a known original operation_ref with operation_get; if lost, discover mode=ifc_products_recovery with "
    "tool_name=ifc_products_edit and match input/time, never merely the latest entry. "
    "mode=recover accepts only that original operation_ref and continues its saved request/claim/budget; it never accepts new input. "
    "For replace, the backend independently evaluates the row's solved named interfaces against declared "
    "conditions and replaces the requested subtree. Placement/delete produce their own action facts, "
    "without new Family interface observations. Establish external relationships from CURRENT facts "
    "and explicitly coordinate affected objects; historical reports retain their original coordinates."
)
